-- EVERY PASS-EXCLUSIVE ITEM IN EVERY CATALOGUE, derived rather than listed.
--
-- The alternative is a `grants` array on each pass row, which is a second
-- copy of a fact the catalogues already carry -- and the failure shape is
-- known and silent: an item tagged `pass = "vip"` but missing from the
-- list is excluded from every roll and granted by nothing, so it exists,
-- borders as legendary, and is unobtainable by any route. Deriving cannot
-- produce that state.
--
-- IT WALKS `Config.catalogueFor`'S OWN KINDS, so a seventh catalogue is
-- covered the moment it is added there rather than the session somebody
-- notices. Returns `{kind, key}` pairs, which is the grammar
-- `SetService.grant` already speaks.
function Config.passItems(passKey: string): { { kind: string, key: string } }
	local out = {}
	for _, kind in ipairs(Config.COSMETIC_KINDS) do
		local catalogue = Config.catalogueFor(kind)
		if catalogue then
			for key, spec in pairs(catalogue) do
				if type(spec) == "table" and spec.pass == passKey then
					table.insert(out, { kind = kind, key = key })
				end
			end
		end
	end
	-- Sorted, so a grant announces its items in the same order every time
	-- rather than in whatever order `pairs` happened to walk two tables.
	table.sort(out, function(a, b)
		if a.kind ~= b.kind then
			return a.kind < b.kind
		end
		return a.key < b.key
	end)
	return out
end

-- HOW FAST THE SIDEWAYS STANCE CATCHES UP TO A NEW HEADING, in degrees per
-- second, never applied instantly.
--
-- Only a ride with a `bodyYaw` turns the root at all -- see the note over
-- RIDE_YAW_ATTRIBUTE in RideService -- and today that is the skateboard alone.
-- The first version set the root's CFrame straight to the target heading
-- every frame, which is exactly right for WHERE to face and wrong for HOW to
-- get there: a keyboard's MoveDirection is not continuous, it is one of eight
-- fixed vectors that changes in a single frame the instant A becomes D, so the
-- board spun on the spot rather than carving into the new direction.
--
-- 420 completes a full reversal (A to D, 180 degrees) in just under half a
-- second -- fast enough to stay responsive in a chase, slow enough that
-- consecutive frames differ by a few degrees rather than by however far the
-- input happened to turn. ClientMain eases the ROOT toward this target at
-- this rate every frame; RideYaw itself is still the instant, published fact
-- of which way the deck is meant to sit.
-- DEGREES PER SECOND, NOT STUDS -- an ANGULAR rate, so it is deliberately
-- not a ratio of `BASE_WALK_SPEED`: how fast a deck swings round under a
-- rider has nothing to do with how far they cover in a second.
Config.RIDE_STANCE_TURN_RATE = 420

-- HOW FAST A RIDE GOES IS A PROPERTY OF ITS TIER, NEVER OF ITS OWN ROW.
--
-- Every ride carried its own `multiplier`, and six numbers that are meant to
-- agree in pairs are six numbers that drift. Measured before the change, five
-- of the six ALREADY agreed with the tier they sit in -- the only pair that
-- disagreed was the two commons, the Skateboard at 1.35 against the BMX at
-- 1.50 -- so this is very nearly a description of what the catalogue already
-- was rather than a re-tune of it.
--
-- WHAT IT MEANS FOR A PURCHASE, stated plainly, because it is a real change:
-- inside a tier the cheaper ride is now strictly the better buy, and what the
-- dearer one sells is its LOOK, its sound and its stance. That is consistent
-- with the rule that a ride confers nothing else, and it makes the shop honest
-- in the one number a ride is actually sold on -- a rider can read the tier
-- border and know the speed, instead of comparing two prices to find out that
-- they bought the same thing.
--
-- THE SKATEBOARD WENT UP RATHER THAN THE BMX COMING DOWN. Equalising downward
-- would have taken speed off a 90,000-coin purchase somebody had already made,
-- which is the one direction this project does not move: nothing here takes
-- away what was bought. Note it makes the entry ride quicker, so the measured
-- "end to end is 14.8s on the 25,000 skateboard" in CLAUDE.md is now 13.3s.
--
-- AND IT COUPLES A PRICE TO A SPEED THROUGH `rarityOf`, which is worth knowing
-- before anybody edits a cost. A ride's tier is derived from its price for
-- every row that has one, so moving a price across a `RARITY_BANDS` border now
-- moves that ride's speed with it. That is the intended reading of "a tier is a
-- speed" and it is still a coupling: the audit below is what says so out loud.
Config.RIDE_TIER_SPEED = {
	common = 1.5,
	rare = 1.65,
	epic = 1.8,
	legendary = 2.1,
}

-- The walk-speed multiplier a ride is worth. One place, so the tier ladder
-- above cannot be contradicted by a row.
function Config.rideMultiplier(spec: any): number
	return Config.RIDE_TIER_SPEED[Config.rarityOf(spec)] or 1
end

